unit_text:
Try this code: #This function is supposed to 'work' after 3 turns def action(current_turn, action_turn): if global_turn != action_turn: return print('action!') game = True global_turn = 0 action_turn = None while game: global_turn += 1 print(f'
This is turn #{global_turn}
') user_input = input('Do [1]yes [2]no') if user_input == '2': pass elif user_input == '1': action_turn = global_turn + 3 action(global_turn, action_turn) Note: this does not actually run a while loop in the background. In your example it does not seem necessary. But if your game becomes more complex you might want to look into threading (https://docs.python.org/3/library/threading.html) multiprocessing (https://docs.python.org/3/library/multiprocessing.html) asyncio (https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html) to run things in the background. EDIT An asyncio (https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio.html) version: import asyncio class Game: def __init__(self): self.game_running = True self.global_turn = 0 self.turn_started = asyncio.Event() self.turn_ended = asyncio.Event() async def action(self, wait_turns): while wait_turns > 0: await self.turn_ended.wait() await self.turn_started.wait() wait_turns -= 1 print('action!') async def main(self): while self.game_running: self.global_turn += 1 print(f'
This is turn #{self.global_turn}
') self.turn_ended.clear() self.turn_started.set() await asyncio.sleep(0) # yield control to other tasks user_input = input('Do [1]yes [2]no') if user_input == '2': pass

